2186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492815 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb692d57-2d39-5d74-a61f-7f4cc771ea13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,6)$ in slope-intercept form.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1abe194d-a2aa-502b-8bc6-8f34c848d468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492835 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-(-2)}{1-(-1)}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=4$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =4(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+2 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =4(x+1) \\\\ y+2 & =4x+4 \\\\ y & =4x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214702" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1071 "Express the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,3)$ and has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in point-slope form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}\n$($\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22f6fedf-943a-57f1-98a3-a82b2489035f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492841 #edb/ref 1074 "Using $m=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,3)$, we substitute into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,-8)$ and $(8,-6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e072874-ed7d-544d-b970-83b9972e3853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492843 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-6-(-8)}{8-4}=\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-8)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-8) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+8 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+8 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-2-8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-208825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(3,9)$ and $(-1,1)$ in slope-intercept form.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72696385-5600-5d16-af0f-e8ec844daceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492871 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-9}{-1-3}=\\frac{(-8)}{(-4)}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=2$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,9)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-9 & =2(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =2(x-3) \\\\ y-9 & =2x-6 \\\\ y & =2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,-5)$ and $(6,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "807e0d45-6dda-516b-87e0-9ddf87246055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492881 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the line passes through the point $(2,-5),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-5)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-3-(-5)}{6-2}=\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-5)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(1,-1)$ in slope-intercept form.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99417d8d-1f43-5c3e-b523-0e838b0c9470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492886 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-3}{1-(-1)}=\\frac{-4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-2$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,3)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x-2 \\\\ y & =-2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the point $(4,1)$ and has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af8f01ea-2536-5ff7-99ba-8a19dc049226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492895 #edb/ref 1074 "Using $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,1)$, we substitute into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,4)$  and $(-4,-8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1c536e5-efa9-5d3b-97fb-617b507bd5a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492910 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the line passes through the point $(2,4),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{(-8)-4}{(-4)-2}=\\frac{-12}{-6}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=2$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =2(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
